# Postgres for the self-hosted Supabase data plane: pgvector, plus pg_cron.
#
# Why this image exists
# ---------------------
# The stack used pgvector/pgvector:pg16 directly. That image carries the vector
# extension and nothing else, so `pg_available_extensions` never listed pg_cron
# and 20260729_02_metering_shadow_verdicts_retention.sql took its documented
# skip branch on every boot of this deployment: config table created, purge
# procedure created, and no nightly schedule at all. Nothing surfaced that,
# because the deploy's retention report was reading the hosted Supabase project
# (which does preload pg_cron) rather than the database the stack uses. The
# hosted project is now deleted, so the real state is visible: retention has
# never been scheduled here.
#
# Making the extension available is therefore a property of the image, not of a
# hand-run CREATE EXTENSION on the box. A container recreate, a fresh volume, or
# a rebuilt box all get pg_cron because it is baked in here and preloaded by the
# `command:` in docker-compose.enterprise.yml.
#
# Why a derived image and not supabase/postgres
# ---------------------------------------------
# supabase/postgres bundles pgvector and pg_cron together and would be one line
# of compose. It was rejected for this deployment on two counts, both already
# recorded in this repository rather than guessed at:
#
#   * The live data directory was initdb'd by pgvector/pgvector:pg16, which is
#     the official postgres image plus one extension. supabase/postgres ships a
#     different configuration layout, its own roles, and its own init scripts,
#     so pointing it at an existing data directory is a migration, not an image
#     swap. This database holds the live billing ledger.
#   * .github/ci/test-db-bootstrap.sql records what happened when that image was
#     tried as CI's throwaway database: its `postgres` role is not a superuser,
#     which forced a second connection as supabase_admin for pg_cron and for
#     auth-schema writes.
#
# This image is the same base with one extra package, so the data directory, the
# entrypoint, the roles and the superuser story are all unchanged.
#
# The base is pinned by digest, same value the compose file used to name.
# Refresh: docker pull pgvector/pgvector:pg16 && docker inspect --format='{{index .RepoDigests 0}}' pgvector/pgvector:pg16
FROM pgvector/pgvector:pg16@sha256:131dcf7ff6a900545df8e7e092c270aa8c6db2f2c818e408cb45ec21316b74e6

# postgresql-16-cron comes from the PGDG apt repository the official postgres
# image already configures (bookworm-pgdg main 16), so this needs no new source
# and no key handling. Deliberately unpinned: PGDG drops superseded versions
# from the repository, so a pinned version turns into a build that cannot
# resolve at all a few months from now, which is a worse failure than a minor
# version drift in a scheduler.
#
# ponytail: one package, no build stage, no compile from source. pg_cron is
# packaged for this exact base; building it would be strictly more to maintain.
#
# The two `test -f` lines fail the build, rather than the first CREATE
# EXTENSION, if the package ever stops shipping the files that make the
# extension available. A control file that is not on disk is exactly what made
# 20260729_02 skip silently in the first place.
RUN set -eux; \
    apt-get update; \
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends postgresql-16-cron; \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*; \
    test -f /usr/share/postgresql/16/extension/pg_cron.control; \
    test -f /usr/lib/postgresql/16/lib/pg_cron.so
